  "body": "Soreness entries were keyed by date alone with no reference to the workout that caused them, so there was no way to see how long a session's soreness actually lasted.\n\n## Model\n\nAn entry is now identified by **(date, source workout)**. You log against the same workout repeatedly as the soreness fades, and that sequence *is* its recovery curve. Two workouts' soreness can overlap on the same day without fighting over one record.\n\nUnattributed entries keep the bare `soreness-\u003cdate\u003e` id the old scheme used, so **all 46 existing entries carry over as \"unattributed\" with no migration**.\n\n## What's new\n\n- **Log soreness from a workout** — in the History feed's expanded workout card, and in the workout editor (which is where a calendar click lands).\n- **Source-first editor** — \"+ Log Soreness\" asks which workout caused it, then filters the muscle picker to that day's muscle groups, with a \"show all\" escape hatch. Carry-forward now pulls from the last log for that same workout.\n- **Lanes view** — a vertical date rail where each workout spawns a stripe that runs for as long as its soreness lasted. Toggles for stripe grouping (workout / muscle group), missed days (bridge / break), order, quiet-day collapsing, and date window.\n\nLanes are packed greedily by start date and **never reflow**. This is interval-graph colouring: the minimum lane count equals peak concurrency and greedy-by-start achieves it, so shifting a live stripe to backfill a freed lane buys zero width while destroying the stable x-position that makes a stripe's length readable. A freed lane staying empty is itself information.\n\n## Reviewer notes\n\n- **`frontend/public/snapshot.db` is a deliberate binary change.** The snapshot schema gains `id` and `source_workout_*` columns; the committed db is migrated in place so anonymous visitors don't hit `no such column: id` before the next 4-hour cron regeneration. Please don't revert it as noise.\n- `DELETE /api/soreness/:date` → `/api/soreness/:id`, back-compatible — a bare date still resolves to that date's unattributed entry.\n\n## Verification\n\n- 17 assertions over the curve/lane logic, including overlapping recoveries from two workouts on the same dates, and a DST-boundary date check\n- Anonymous snapshot path exercised directly: reader parses the new schema, all 46 legacy entries carry ids, Lanes handles zero-attributed data without crashing\n- Save path confirmed round-tripping against the live API\n- Screenshotted desktop and 390px mobile for every view; repo guard scripts (`check-removed-msal`, `check-image-fingerprint-config`) pass\n\n## Known follow-ups\n\n- The **Timeline** view is now redundant with Lanes (same idea, day-offset columns instead of absolute dates, so its lanes aren't comparable). Deleting it would also retire `buildRecoveryCurves` and `muscleKey`.\n- Soreness cards in the feed still file under the date felt, not under their source workout — deliberate, open to change.\n- Mobile right-edge clipping on the History stats strip is **pre-existing** (confirmed by stashing); untouched here.\n",
